    },
    {
      "id": "overview",
      "title": "Overview",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Ludwig van Beethoven (1770–1827) bridged Classical and Romantic styles, expanding emotional range and formal scale."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "innovations",
      "title": "Innovations",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Nine symphonies including the iconic Fifth (short-short-short-long motive). Late string quartets pushed harmonic boundaries. Deafness from his 30s did not stop composition — he 'heard' internally."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "motivic-development",
      "title": "Motivic development",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Short rhythmic or melodic cells drive entire movements. Fifth Symphony opening = four-note fate motive developed across the work."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "cultural-impact",
      "title": "Cultural impact",
      "level": 2,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "markdown",
          "text": "Music as personal expression, not mere entertainment. Echoes in folk revival movements claiming repertoire ownership."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
